{"step_index":16,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:57:17Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:57:04Z sender=9e162f78-e95f-47da-b0ba-af19f50adfd4 priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=---**I INSTABILITY TESTS**---**1 APPREHENSION TEST** Purpose To assess anterior shoulder instability Procedure Abduct the shoulder to 90 flex the elbow to 90 and slowly externally rotate the shoulder Positive Sign Patient becomes apprehensive or fears shoulder dislocation Indicates Anterior glenohumeral instability---**2 JERK TEST** Purpose To assess posterior shoulder instability and posterior labral lesions Procedure Shoulder abducted to 90 and internally rotated Apply axial compression through the elbow while horizontally adducting the arm Positive Sign Painful clunk or jerk Indicates Posterior instability or posterior labral tear---**3 DRAWER TEST** Purpose To assess anterior and posterior glenohumeral instability Procedure Stabilize the scapula and translate the humeral head anteriorly and posteriorly Positive Sign Excessive humeral head movement compared to the opposite side Indicates Shoulder instability---**4 SULCUS SIGN** Purpose To assess inferior glenohumeral instability Procedure Apply downward traction to the relaxed arm Positive Sign Visible sulcus below the acromion Indicates Inferior or multidirectional shoulder instability---**II IMPINGEMENT TESTS**---**1 HAWKINS KENNEDY TEST** Purpose Assess subacromial impingement Procedure Flex shoulder and elbow to 90 and internally rotate the shoulder Positive Sign Pain Indicates Subacromial impingement---**2 NEERS IMPINGEMENT TEST** Purpose Assess subacromial impingement Procedure Stabilize the scapula and passively flex the internally rotated arm Positive Sign Pain Indicates Subacromial impingement---**3 PAINFUL ARC TEST** Purpose Assess shoulder impingement Procedure Ask the patient to actively abduct the shoulder Positive Sign Pain between 60 120 of abduction Indicates Subacromial impingement---**4 EMPTY CAN JOBES TEST** Purpose Assess supraspinatus tendon Procedure Shoulder abducted to 90 in the scapular plane internally rotated thumb down resist downward pressure Positive Sign Pain or weakness Indicates Supraspinatus tendinopathy or tear---**5 DROP ARM TEST** Purpose Assess rotator cuff tear Procedure Passively abduct the arm and ask the patient to slowly lower it Positive Sign Arm drops suddenly or cannot be lowered smoothly Indicates Supraspinatus tear/Rotator cuff tear---**III LABRAL LESION TESTS**---**1 ACTIVE COMPRESSION TEST OBRIEN TEST** Purpose Assess SLAP lesion and AC joint pathology Procedure Shoulder flexed to 90 adducted 10 15 Thumb pointing downward Apply downward resistance Repeat with palm facing upward Positive Sign Pain or clicking that decreases with the palm up position Indicates SLAP lesion or AC joint pathology---**2 KIM TEST** Purpose Assess posteroinferior labral lesion Procedure Shoulder abducted to 90 Apply axial compression with upward and posterior force while elevating the arm diagonally Positive Sign Posterior shoulder pain Indicates Posteroinferior labral tear---**IV SCAPULAR DYSKINESIA TESTS**---**1 SCAPULAR DYSKINESIS TEST** Purpose Assess abnormal scapular movement Procedure Patient performs repeated shoulder flexion and abduction with light weights while the examiner observes scapular motion Positive Sign Scapular winging or asymmetrical movement Indicates Scapular dyskinesia---**2 SCAPULAR LOAD TEST SCAPULAR ASSISTANCE TEST** Purpose Assess the effect of scapular assistance on shoulder movement Procedure Examiner manually assists upward rotation and posterior tilt of the scapula during arm elevation Positive Sign Pain decreases or shoulder movement improves Indicates Scapular dyskinesia---**V MUSCLE PATHOLOGY TESTS**---**1 YERGASONS TEST** Purpose Assess biceps tendon pathology Procedure Patient supinates the forearm against resistance with the elbow flexed to 90 Positive Sign Pain or snapping over the bicipital groove Indicates Biceps tendinitis or transverse humeral ligament injury---**2 SPEEDS TEST** Purpose Assess long head of biceps tendon Procedure Shoulder flexed to 90 elbow extended forearm supinated resist downward pressure Positive Sign Pain in the bicipital groove Indicates Biceps tendinitis or SLAP lesion---**3 LIFT OFF TEST** Purpose Assess Subscapularis muscle Procedure Place the dorsum of the hand on the lower back and ask the patient to lift the hand away Positive Sign Unable to lift the hand or marked weakness Indicates Subscapularis tear or weakness---**4 TEST FOR SUPRASPINATUS** Purpose Assess supraspinatus muscle Procedure Perform the Empty Can Test against resistance Positive Sign Pain or weakness Indicates Supraspinatus pathology---**5 TEST FOR INFRASPINATUS** Purpose Assess infraspinatus muscle Procedure Patient externally rotates the shoulder against resistance with the elbow flexed to 90 Positive Sign Pain or weakness Indicates Infraspinatus tear or weakness---**6 HORNBLOWERS SIGN** Purpose Assess Teres Minor function Procedure Shoulder elevated to 90 in the scapular plane with elbow flexed to 90 Ask the patient to maintain external rotation Positive Sign Unable to maintain the position or external rotation Indicates Teres minor tear or severe rotator cuff pathology\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
{"step_index":17,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:57:17Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:57:06Z sender=a7e55962-d1c0-4e42-9dcc-d29979c73d47 priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=---**I TESTS FOR LATERAL EPICONDYLITIS TENNIS ELBOW** ---**1 COZENS TEST** Purpose To assess lateral epicondylitis Tennis Elbow Procedure • Patient makes a fist • Elbow flexed to 90 • Forearm pronated • Wrist extended and radially deviated against resistance Positive Sign Pain over the lateral epicondyle Indicates Lateral epicondylitis ---**2 MILLS TEST** Purpose To assess lateral epicondylitis Procedure • Patient sits with the elbow extended • Forearm pronated • Therapist passively flexes the wrist while maintaining elbow extension Positive Sign Pain over the lateral epicondyle Indicates Lateral epicondylitis ---**3 MAUDSLEYS TEST MIDDLE FINGER TEST** Purpose To assess lateral epicondylitis especially involvement of the Extensor Digitorum Communis EDC Procedure • Elbow extended • Forearm pronated • Patient resists extension of the middle finger Positive Sign Pain over the lateral epicondyle Indicates Lateral epicondylitis ---**II TEST FOR MEDIAL EPICONDYLITIS GOLFERS ELBOW** ---**4 GOLFERS ELBOW TEST MEDIAL EPICONDYLITIS TEST** Purpose To assess medial epicondylitis Golfers Elbow Procedure • Patient sits with the elbow extended • Forearm supinated • Therapist passively extends the wrist and fingers while maintaining elbow extension Positive Sign Pain over the medial epicondyle Indicates Medial Epicondylitis Golfers Elbow ---**III NERVE COMPRESSION TEST** ---**5 TINELS SIGN CUBITAL TUNNEL** Purpose To assess ulnar nerve irritation or compression Procedure • Slightly flex the elbow • Tap gently over the ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle Positive Sign Tingling or electric shock sensation along the ring and little fingers Indicates Cubital Tunnel Syndrome Ulnar nerve entrapment ---**IV LIGAMENT STABILITY TESTS** ---**6 VALGUS STRESS TEST** Purpose To assess the Medial Ulnar Collateral Ligament MCL/UCL Procedure • Elbow slightly flexed 20 30 • Stabilize the humerus • Apply a valgus force to the forearm Positive Sign Pain or excessive medial joint opening Indicates Medial collateral ligament injury ---**7 VARUS STRESS TEST** Purpose To assess the Lateral Collateral Ligament LCL Procedure • Elbow slightly flexed 20 30 • Stabilize the humerus • Apply a varus force to the forearm Positive Sign Pain or excessive lateral joint opening Indicates Lateral collateral ligament injury\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
{"step_index":18,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:57:17Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:57:12Z sender=b0b6fc47-18db-4c28-91e7-af9ea5ee9dcd priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=--- **WRIST SPECIAL TESTS** --- **I CARPAL TUNNEL SYNDROME TESTS** --- **1 PHALENS TEST** Purpose To assess Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Median nerve compression Procedure • Ask the patient to flex both wrists maximally • Dorsum of both hands are pressed together • Hold for 60 seconds Positive Sign Pain tingling or numbness in the thumb index middle and lateral half of ring finger Indicates Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **2 REVERSE PHALENS TEST PRAYER TEST** Purpose To assess Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Procedure • Ask the patient to press both palms together • Maintain maximum wrist extension for 60 seconds Positive Sign Pain or paresthesia in the median nerve distribution Indicates Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **3 TINELS SIGN WRIST** Purpose To assess Median nerve irritation Procedure • Tap gently over the median nerve at the carpal tunnel Positive Sign Tingling or electric shock sensation in the median nerve distribution Indicates Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **II TENDON PATHOLOGY TEST** --- **4 FINKELSTEINS TEST** Purpose To assess De Quervains Tenosynovitis Procedure • Patient places the thumb inside the fist • Fingers wrap around the thumb • Examiner passively deviates the wrist towards the ulnar side Positive Sign Pain over the radial styloid Indicates De Quervains Tenosynovitis --- **III LIGAMENT STABILITY TEST** --- **5 WATSON SCAPHOID SHIFT TEST** Purpose To assess Scapholunate ligament instability Procedure • Apply pressure over the scaphoid tubercle • Move the wrist from ulnar deviation to radial deviation Positive Sign Pain or palpable clunk Indicates Scapholunate ligament injury --- **IV THUMB TEST** --- **6 GRIND TEST** Purpose To assess 1st Carpometacarpal CMC joint osteoarthritis Procedure • Apply axial compression to the thumb • Simultaneously rotate the thumb metacarpal Positive Sign Pain or crepitus Indicates 1st CMC joint osteoarthritis --- **I TESTS FOR BONE LIGAMENT CAPSULE JOINT INSTABILITY** --- **1 ULNAR FOVEA TEST** Purpose To assess Triangular Fibrocartilage Complex TFCC injury Procedure • Press the examiners thumb into the ulnar fovea between the ulnar styloid and flexor carpi ulnaris tendon Positive Sign Pain or tenderness over the ulnar fovea Indicates TFCC injury or ulnotriquetral ligament injury --- **2 ULNO MENISCO TRIQUETRAL DORSAL GLIDE TEST** Purpose To assess ulnotriquetral ligament and TFCC pathology Procedure • Stabilize the distal ulna • Apply a dorsal glide to the triquetrum while assessing pain and movement Positive Sign Pain or excessive movement Indicates Ulnotriquetral ligament injury or TFCC lesion --- **3 BUNNELL LITTLER TEST** Purpose To differentiate intrinsic muscle tightness from joint capsule tightness Procedure • Flex the PIP joint with the MCP joint extended • Repeat with the MCP joint flexed Positive Sign • PIP flexion improves when MCP is flexed Intrinsic muscle tightness • PIP flexion remains limited Capsular tightness Indicates Intrinsic muscle contracture or capsular restriction\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
{"step_index":19,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:57:17Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:57:13Z sender=5263ee06-8b32-49ca-9765-df839f9ee61c priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=---**SPECIAL TESTS KNEE JOINT** ---**I TESTS FOR LIGAMENT INTEGRITY** ---**A COLLATERAL LIGAMENTS** ---**1 VALGUS STRESS TEST** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the medial collateral ligament MCL PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Knee is slightly flexed approximately 20 30 to unlock the joint • Stabilize the lateral aspect of the knee • Apply a valgus force to the lower leg while stabilizing the thigh POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive medial joint opening compared with the opposite side • Pain along the medial knee INDICATES MCL injury/insufficiency ---**2 VARUS STRESS TEST** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the lateral collateral ligament LCL PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Knee is slightly flexed approximately 20 30 • Stabilize the medial aspect of the knee • Apply a varus force to the lower leg POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive lateral joint opening • Pain along the lateral knee INDICATES LCL injury/insufficiency ---**B CRUCIATE LIGAMENTS** ---**3 LACHMAN TEST** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the anterior cruciate ligament ACL PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Knee flexed approximately 20 30 • Stabilize the femur with one hand • Hold the proximal tibia with the other hand • Pull the tibia anteriorly POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive anterior translation of tibia • Soft/absent firm end feel compared with the normal side INDICATES ACL injury/tear Very important Lachman is generally more sensitive for ACL injury than the anterior drawer test particularly in the acute knee ---**4 ANTERIOR DRAWER TEST** PURPOSE To assess the ACL PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Hip flexed and knee flexed to 90 • Stabilize the foot • Grasp the proximal tibia with both hands • Pull the tibia anteriorly POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive anterior movement of the tibia compared with the opposite side INDICATES ACL injury/insufficiency ---**5 POSTERIOR DRAWER TEST** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the posterior cruciate ligament PCL PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Knee flexed to approximately 90 • Stabilize the foot • Place both hands around the proximal tibia • Push the tibia posteriorly POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive posterior translation of the tibia INDICATES PCL injury/insufficiency ---**II TESTS FOR MENISCAL INJURY** ---**6 MCMURRAYS TEST** PURPOSE To assess for medial and lateral meniscal lesions PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Fully flex the knee • For medial meniscus externally rotate the tibia and apply valgus stress while extending the knee • For lateral meniscus internally rotate the tibia and apply varus stress while extending the knee • Palpate the joint line POSITIVE SIGN • Pain • Palpable/audible click or clunk at the joint line INDICATES Possible meniscal tear ---**7 APLEYS COMPRESSION DISTRACTION TEST** PURPOSE To differentiate meniscal pathology from ligament pathology PROCEDURE Compression • Patient lies prone • Knee flexed to 90 • Stabilize the thigh • Apply downward compression through the heel • Rotate the tibia internally and externally Distraction • Maintain the same position • Apply traction to the tibia while rotating it POSITIVE SIGN Compression positive • Pain reproduced with compression/rotation Suggests meniscal pathology Distraction positive • Pain reproduced with traction/rotation Suggests ligament/capsular pathology ---**III JOINT EFFUSION PATELLOFEMORAL DYSFUNCTION** ---**8 FLUCTUATION TEST** PURPOSE To detect knee joint effusion particularly larger effusions PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine with the knee relaxed/extended • Examiner compresses the fluid from one side of the knee toward the opposite side • Observe/palpate for a fluid wave POSITIVE SIGN • A palpable/visible fluid wave or fluctuation INDICATES Knee joint effusion ---**9 PATELLAR TAP TEST** PURPOSE To detect knee joint effusion PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine with knee extended • Gently press/compress the suprapatellar region to move fluid beneath the patella • Tap the patella downward against the femur POSITIVE SIGN • Patella rebounds/floats and taps the femur producing a palpable tap INDICATES Significant knee joint effusion ---**10 CLARKES TEST / PATELLAR GRIND TEST** PURPOSE To assess patellofemoral dysfunction particularly chondromalacia/patellofemoral pain PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine with the knee extended • Examiner places the hand proximal to the patella • Apply gentle downward pressure to the patella • Ask the patient to contract the quadriceps POSITIVE SIGN • Pain behind/around the patella • Inability to maintain quadriceps contraction because of pain INDICATES Possible patellofemoral dysfunction/chondral pathology ---**IV OTHER MEASUREMENT** ---**11 Q ANGLE** PURPOSE To assess patellofemoral alignment and the lateral pull on the patella MEASUREMENT Q angle is formed by the intersection of • Line 1 ASIS centre of patella • Line 2 centre of patella tibial tubercle PROCEDURE • Patient positioned with the knee in the appropriate standardized position • Identify and mark • ASIS • Centre of patella • Tibial tubercle • Use a goniometer to measure the angle between the two lines POSITIVE/ABNORMAL FINDING An increased Q angle may indicate increased lateral patellar alignment/pull and may be associated with patellofemoral dysfunction INDICATES • Patellofemoral malalignment • Predisposition to lateral patellar tracking/instability\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
