{"step_index":21,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:57:51Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:57:31Z sender=5e38b999-b288-4523-a3fd-900c6ee6a19e priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=**SPECIAL TESTS LUMBO SACRAL REGION** --- **I TEST FOR LUMBAR INSTABILITY** --- **1 PASSIVE LUMBAR EXTENSION TEST** PURPOSE To assess lumbar spine instability PROCEDURE • Patient lies prone • Examiner lifts both the patients legs together by holding around the ankles • The legs are elevated while the knees remain extended • Observe the patients symptoms and lumbar response POSITIVE SIGN • Development/reproduction of low back pain • Feeling of heaviness or instability in the lumbar region INDICATES Lumbar segmental instability --- **II TESTS FOR MUSCLE DYSFUNCTION** --- **2 90 90 STRAIGHT LEG RAISING TEST** PURPOSE To assess hamstring muscle tightness/flexibility PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Flex the hip and knee to approximately 90 • Stabilize the thigh • Slowly extend the knee while maintaining hip flexion POSITIVE SIGN • Inability to achieve full knee extension • Increased resistance/tightness in the posterior thigh INDICATES Hamstring tightness/shortness --- **3 TRENDELENBURG TEST** PURPOSE To assess hip abductor muscle function especially gluteus medius PROCEDURE • Patient stands on one leg • Ask the patient to lift the opposite foot from the floor • Observe the position of the pelvis POSITIVE SIGN • Pelvis drops on the side of the lifted/non weight bearing leg INDICATES Weakness of the hip abductors of the weight bearing leg particularly gluteus medius --- **III TESTS FOR NEUROLOGICAL DYSFUNCTION** --- **4 STRAIGHT LEG RAISING TEST SLR/LASÈGUES TEST** PURPOSE To assess lumbosacral nerve root irritation particularly due to PIVD PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Keep the knee fully extended • Slowly raise the patients straight leg by flexing the hip • Note the angle and symptoms produced POSITIVE SIGN • Reproduction of radicular/sciatic pain typically between about 30 70 of hip flexion INDICATES Lumbosacral nerve root irritation/compression commonly associated with PIVD --- **5 SLUMP TEST** PURPOSE To assess neural tissue mechanosensitivity particularly lumbosacral nerve root involvement PROCEDURE Patient sits with 1 Hands behind back 2 Thoracic/lumbar spine flexed 3 Cervical spine flexed 4 One knee is extended 5 Ankle is dorsiflexed The examiner progressively adds these components while observing symptoms POSITIVE SIGN • Reproduction of the patients familiar neural/radicular symptoms • Symptoms change with release of one component particularly cervical extension or ankle movement INDICATES Neural tissue/nerve root involvement such as lumbar radiculopathy --- **IV TESTS FOR JOINT DYSFUNCTION** --- **6 SCHOBERS TEST** PURPOSE To assess lumbar spine flexion/mobility PROCEDURE • Patient stands erect • Mark the lumbosacral region and a point 10 cm above it • Ask the patient to bend forward maximally • Measure the distance between the marks again POSITIVE SIGN Insufficient increase in distance during forward flexion INDICATES Reduced lumbar flexion/mobility commonly seen in conditions such as ankylosing spondylitis --- **7 FABER / PATRICKS TEST** FABER Flexion ABduction External Rotation PURPOSE To assess hip and sacroiliac joint pathology PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Place the tested leg in • Hip flexion • Abduction • External rotation • Place the lateral ankle over the opposite thigh • Stabilize the opposite pelvis • Gently press the flexed knee downward POSITIVE SIGN • Pain reproduced in the hip/groin suggests hip pathology • Pain in the sacroiliac region may indicate SI joint pathology • Restricted ROM may also be significant INDICATES Hip or sacroiliac joint dysfunction --- **8 GAENSLENS TEST** PURPOSE To assess sacroiliac joint dysfunction/pathology PROCEDURE • Patient lies near the edge of the table • One hip and knee are maximally flexed toward the chest • The opposite leg is allowed to hang off the edge of the table • Apply gentle pressure to produce opposite movements at the pelvis POSITIVE SIGN • Reproduction of pain in the sacroiliac region INDICATES Sacroiliac joint dysfunction --- **9 GILLETS TEST** PURPOSE To assess sacroiliac joint mobility PROCEDURE • Patient stands • Examiner palpates the PSIS and sacrum • Patient flexes one hip and knee lifting the foot from the floor • Observe movement of the PSIS relative to the sacrum POSITIVE SIGN • Abnormal or restricted movement of the PSIS compared with the opposite side INDICATES Sacroiliac joint hypomobility/dysfunction --- **10 MARCHS TEST** PURPOSE To assess sacroiliac joint movement/dysfunction PROCEDURE • Patient stands • Examiner palpates the PSIS • Patient marches by lifting one knee at a time • Observe the movement of the PSIS POSITIVE SIGN • Abnormal/asymmetrical movement of the PSIS INDICATES Sacroiliac joint dysfunction or altered mobility\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
{"step_index":22,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:57:51Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:57:32Z sender=8aa9bbc8-11eb-4ae4-afd7-3c10ae615a0e priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=**CERVICAL SPECIAL TESTS** --- **1 Spurlings Test /Foraminal Compression Test/** **PURPOSE** To assess cervical nerve root compression /cervical radiculopathy/ **PROCEDURE** • Patient sits comfortably • Extend laterally flex and rotate the head towards the affected side • Apply gentle downward compression on the head **POSITIVE SIGN** Radiating pain numbness or tingling into the upper limb **INDICATES** • Cervical nerve root compression • Cervical radiculopathy --- **2 Cervical Distraction Test** **PURPOSE** To assess cervical nerve root compression **PROCEDURE** • Patient lies supine • Therapist gently grasps the head and applies an upward traction force **POSITIVE SIGN** Relief of neck or arm pain **INDICATES** • Cervical radiculopathy • Foraminal compression --- **3 Upper Limb Tension Test /ULTT/** **PURPOSE** To assess neural tissue mobility and detect peripheral nerve or cervical nerve root involvement --- **ULTT 1 /MEDIAN NERVE BIAS/** **Procedure** • Depress the shoulder • Abduct the shoulder to 110 • Supinate the forearm • Extend the wrist and fingers • Externally rotate the shoulder • Slowly extend the elbow • Finally side flex the neck away if required **Positive Sign** • Pain tingling or numbness along the median nerve distribution • Difference in symptoms compared to the opposite side **Indicates** • Median nerve tension • Cervical radiculopathy --- **ULTT 2A /MEDIAN NERVE BIAS/** **Procedure** • Shoulder depression • Elbow extension • External rotation of shoulder • Forearm supination • Wrist and finger extension **Positive Sign** Reproduction of median nerve symptoms **Indicates** Median nerve irritation --- **ULTT 2B /RADIAL NERVE BIAS/** **Procedure** • Shoulder depression • Internal rotation of shoulder • Elbow extension • Forearm pronation • Wrist finger and thumb flexion with ulnar deviation **Positive Sign** Pain or tingling along the dorsolateral forearm and hand **Indicates** Radial nerve tension --- **ULTT 3 /ULNAR NERVE BIAS/** **Procedure** • Shoulder depression • Shoulder abduction and external rotation • Elbow flexion • Forearm pronation • Wrist extension with radial deviation • Finger extension **Positive Sign** Pain or tingling along the medial forearm and little finger **Indicates** Ulnar nerve tension --- **4 Vertebral Artery Test** **PURPOSE** To assess vertebrobasilar insufficiency before cervical mobilization or manipulation **PROCEDURE** • Patient lies supine • Therapist extends rotates and laterally flexes the neck • Hold for about 30 seconds while observing symptoms **POSITIVE SIGN** • Dizziness • Vertigo • Nystagmus • Blurred vision • Nausea **INDICATES** Vertebrobasilar insufficiency **Precaution** Stop the test immediately if symptoms appear --- **SPECIAL EXAMINATION TESTS** --- **1 DIZZINESS TEST** **PURPOSE** To assess for cervical vascular compromise/vertebrobasilar insufficiency that may produce dizziness during cervical movement **PROCEDURE** • Patient is positioned sitting • Examiner stabilizes the patients head • Slowly place the cervical spine into extension and rotation • Observe the patient for symptoms **POSITIVE SIGN** • Dizziness • Vertigo • Nausea • Visual disturbance • Nystagmus **INDICATES** Possible vertebrobasilar insufficiency and requires further medical evaluation --- **2 SHOULDER DEPRESSION TEST** **PURPOSE** To assess nerve root/nerve tissue irritation or shortening of neural structures in the cervical and upper limb region **PROCEDURE** • Patient is sitting • Examiner stabilizes/depresses the shoulder on the tested side • Passively side flex the patients neck away from the tested side • The position places tension on the cervical nerve structures **POSITIVE SIGN** • Reproduction of the patients radiating pain/paresthesia into the upper limb **INDICATES** Possible cervical nerve root or brachial plexus involvement --- **3 PINCH GRIP TEST** **PURPOSE** To assess the function of the anterior interosseous nerve /AIN/ and flexor pollicis longus/index FDP function **PROCEDURE** • Ask the patient to make a pinch/OK sign by touching the tip of the thumb to the tip of the index finger • Observe the shape of the circle formed **POSITIVE SIGN** • Patient cannot make a proper round OK circle • Instead the pinch becomes flattened/pulp to pulp **INDICATES** Anterior interosseous nerve palsy/dysfunction --- **4 CARPAL COMPRESSION TEST** **PURPOSE** To assess for carpal tunnel syndrome due to median nerve compression **PROCEDURE** • Patients wrist is in a neutral position • Examiner applies direct pressure over the carpal tunnel with the thumbs/fingers • Maintain pressure for approximately 30 seconds **POSITIVE SIGN** • Reproduction of • Pain • Tingling • Numbness • Paresthesia in the median nerve distribution **INDICATES** Carpal Tunnel Syndrome / median nerve compression --- **5 HAND ELEVATION TEST** **PURPOSE** To assess for carpal tunnel syndrome/median nerve compression **PROCEDURE** • Patient raises both hands above the head • Maintain the elevated position for approximately 1 2 minutes • Ask the patient about symptoms **POSITIVE SIGN** • Reproduction of pain numbness or tingling in the median nerve distribution **INDICATES** Possible Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **6 FROMENTS SIGN** **PURPOSE** To assess the ulnar nerve particularly the function of adductor pollicis **PROCEDURE** • Ask the patient to hold a piece of paper between the thumb and index finger • Examiner attempts to pull the paper away **POSITIVE SIGN** Patient compensates by • Flexing the IP joint of the thumb using flexor pollicis longus **INDICATES** Ulnar nerve palsy/weakness of adductor pollicis\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
